\section*{Diameter, local mass and covering exponents of fixed-length honeycomb walks}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Uniform honeycomb walks of every sufficiently large length are claimed to have diameter scale $3/4$, while local counts and covers simultaneously have exponent $4/3$, with a polynomial failure bound.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} Let \(\gamma\) be uniform among length-\(n\) self-avoiding honeycomb-lattice walks from a fixed vertex, with unrestricted endpoint. Write \(D(\gamma)\) for Euclidean diameter, \(M_\gamma(z,s)\) for the number of visited vertices in the closed radius-\(s\) ball at \(z\), and \(\mathcal N_\gamma(s)\) for the minimum number of such balls covering the visited set. For every \(\delta,k>0\), the source supplies \(C_{\delta,k}<\infty\) and \(n_0\), so every \(n\ge n_0\) has an event of probability at least \(1-C_{\delta,k}n^{-k}\) on which
\[
 n^{3/4-\delta}\le D(\gamma)\le n^{3/4+\delta},
\]
\[
 n^{-\delta}\min\{n,s^{4/3}\}\le M_\gamma(z,s)\le n^\delta\min\{n,s^{4/3}\},
\]
\[
 n^{-\delta}(1+ns^{-4/3})\le\mathcal N_\gamma(s)\le n^\delta(1+ns^{-4/3}).
\]
The latter bounds hold simultaneously for all visited \(z\) and every real \(1\le s\le n\).

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Critical strip, nesting, polygon, and arc inputs bound path masses in boxes. Localized bridges with recoverable seams permit conditioning on exact lengths; multiscale covering and local-visit estimates are then made simultaneous without restricting to a density-one length set.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Uniform honeycomb walks of every sufficiently large length are claimed to have diameter scale $3/4$, while local counts and covers simultaneously have exponent $4/3$, with a polynomial failure bound. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 237, the selected Lean statements give critical-strip mass and displacement exponents, bridge summability, and existence of pulled free energy. The $4/3$ bridge-length and $3/4$ spatial laws and the other polygon/chord exponents are outside these statements. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

